module tools/test_coverage/fixtures/named_only

-- SURVIVOR, and the only fixture that exercises the `named-test` syntactic
-- form on its own. skip_recorded.ail carries both forms, so a mutant deleting
-- the `named-test` pattern would leave it detected and escape; this file has
-- nothing else to be found by, and fires `undetected` the moment the pattern
-- stops matching. A pattern no fixture can lose is a pattern nobody checks.
--
-- WHY THE BODIES ARE BARE ARITHMETIC AND MUST STAY THAT WAY. v0.33.0 runs named
-- test blocks by lifting each body into a synthetic module, and in a file whose
-- ONLY tests are named blocks it never compiles `$builtin` for that module. Any
-- body needing a builtin then dies before it asserts anything:
--
--   test "x" { startsWith("abc", "ab") }  ->  EVA002 ... $builtin._str_startsWith
--   test "x" { contains("abc", "b") }     ->  EVA002 ... $builtin._str_find
--   test "x" { not false }                ->  EVA002 ... $builtin.not_Bool
--
-- Even `not` is a builtin. Only expressions the evaluator folds inline — integer
-- arithmetic and comparison on literals — run at all. skip_recorded.ail's four
-- named blocks call `startsWith` and PASS, because that file also carries a
-- conventional `tests [...]` block, which gets `$builtin` compiled and the
-- lifted bodies then resolve; being named-test-ONLY is precisely what breaks it,
-- and being named-test-ONLY is precisely this fixture's job.
--
-- So the two constraints are in tension on this pin and the resolution is to
-- keep the form and drop the imports. Do not add an import or a stdlib call
-- here, and do not add a `tests [...]` block to "fix" it — the first makes this
-- a permanent `failing` row, the second destroys the only thing it covers. The
-- defect is filed upstream rather than absorbed; when it is fixed, this file can
-- go back to asserting something less trivial.

test "arithmetic identity" { 1 + 1 == 2 }
test "arithmetic product" { 3 * 4 == 12 }
